ird row (or third column) as it contains two zero elements, simplifying the calculation:\n$\\text{det}(M) = 0 \\cdot C_{31} + 0 \\cdot C_{32} + 1 \\cdot C_{33}$\nwhere $C_{33}$ is the cofactor of the element at position (3,3). The minor $M_{33}$ is:\n$M_{33} = \\text{det} \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos \\theta & \\sin \\theta \\\\ \\sin \\theta & \\cos \\theta \\end{pmatrix}$\n$M_{33} = (\\cos \\theta)(\\cos \\theta) - (\\sin \\theta)(\\sin \\theta)$\n$M_{33} = \\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta$\nUsing the double-angle identity, $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta$, we get:\n$M_{33} = \\cos(2\\theta)$\nSince $C_{33} = (-1)^{3+3} M_{33} = M_{33}$, the determinant of M is:\n$\\text{det}(M) = 1 \\cdot \\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)$\n\n**Step 2: Determine the conditions for the rank based on the determinant.**\nThe matrix M is a 3x3 matrix. Its rank is 3 if and only if its determinant is non-zero.\n\n**Case 1: If $\\text{det}(M) \\neq 0$.**\nThis occurs when $\\cos(2\\theta) \\neq 0$.\nGiven that $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi]$, the range for $2\\theta$ is $[0, 4\\pi]$.\n$\\cos(2\\theta) = 0$ when $2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{2}$"
