, -3, 3]$\\n$R_3 \\text{ (new)} = [1 - 1(1), 2 - 1(1), -K - 1(1), 4 - 1(1)] = [0, 1, -K-1, 3]$\\n\\nThe matrix becomes:\\n$C \\sim \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 & : & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & : & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -K-1 & : & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$\\n\\nNow, apply $R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2$:\\n\\n$R_3 \\text{ (new)} = [0 - 0, 1 - 1, (-K-1) - (-3), 3 - 3] = [0, 0, -K-1+3, 0] = [0, 0, 2-K, 0]$\\n\\nThe matrix in Row Echelon Form is:\\n$C \\sim \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 & : & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & : & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-K & : & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$\\n\\n\\n\\nFor a system of linear equations to have more than one solution (infinitely many solutions), two conditions must be met:\\n1.  The system must be consistent, meaning Rank(A) = Rank(C).\\n2.  The rank of the coefficient matrix (A) must be less than the number of variables.\\n\\nIn this system, the number of variables is 3 (x, y, z).\\nFrom the row echelon form, the coefficient matrix A is:\\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-K \\end{bmatrix}$\\n\\nThe augmented matrix C is:\\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 & : & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & : & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-K & : & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$\\n\\nFor more"
