ot \\\\begin{vmatrix} -1 & -1 \\\\\\\\ x & z \\\\end{vmatrix} + 1 \\\\cdot \\\\begin{vmatrix} -1 & 1 \\\\\\\\ x & y \\\\end{vmatrix}$\\\\\n$= 1(1 \\\\cdot z - (-1) \\\\cdot y) + 1((-1) \\\\cdot z - (-1) \\\\cdot x) + 1((-1) \\\\cdot y - 1 \\\\cdot x)$\\\\\n$= 1(z + y) + 1(-z + x) + 1(-y - x)$\\\\\n\\\\\nStep 2: Simplify the expression for $\\\\det A$.\\\\\n$\\\\det A = z + y - z + x - y - x$\\\\\n$\\\\det A = (z - z) + (y - y) + (x - x)$\\\\\n$\\\\det A = 0 + 0 + 0$\\\\\n$\\\\det A = 0$\\\\\n\\\\\nStep 3: Determine the condition for V.\\\\\nThe condition defining V is $\\\\det A = 0$. From our calculation, we found that $\\\\det A = 0$ for any values of x, y, and z.\\\\\nThis means the condition $0 = 0$ holds true for all $(x, y, z) \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^3$.\\\\\n\\\\\nStep 4: Identify the vector space V.\\\\\nSince $\\\\det A = 0$ for all $(x, y, z) \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^3$, the vector space V consists of all vectors in $\\\\mathbb{R}^3$.\\\\\nTherefore, $V = \\\\mathbb{R}^3$.\\\\\n\\\\\nStep 5: Find the dimension of V.\\\\\nThe dimension of the vector space $\\\\mathbb{R}^3$ is 3.\\\\\nThus, $\\\\dim V = 3$.\"}\nRubric for this sample:\n  1. (1 marks) Correctly calculates the determinant of matrix A, showing the intermediate terms before full simplifi"
