one special register (Accumulator) for all data manipulation.\n*    Usually employs 1-address instructions. The address field specifies the memory location of the second operand.\n*    To compute C = A + B:\n    *   `LOAD A` (Contents of memory location A are loaded into AC: AC ← M[A])\n    *   `ADD B` (Contents of memory location B are added to AC: AC ← AC + M[B])\n    *   `STORE C` (Contents of AC are stored into memory location C: M[C] ← AC)\n*    Simple CPU design, shorter instruction format as one operand is implicit.\n*    Requires frequent memory access for loading and storing intermediate results, which can be slow and create a bottleneck.\n\n\nThis is the most widely used organization in modern CPUs. Instead of a single accumulator, it provides a set of multiple  that are accessible to the programmer. These registers can be used to store operands, addresses, or intermediate results, offering much greater flexibility and speed.\n\n*    Utilizes multiple registers (e.g., R0, R1, R2...) for computations.\n*    Can use 2-address or 3-address instructions.\n    *    `OP R1, R2, R3`"
