ng and superscalar execution, which exploit instruction-level parallelism. Examples include most classic single-core CPUs.\n\n**MISD (Multiple Instruction, Single Data)**\nIn an MISD architecture, multiple processing units operate on the same data stream but execute different instruction streams. This means different operations are performed simultaneously on the identical data. This architecture is very rare in practice for general-purpose computing. Potential applications include fault-tolerant systems, where multiple processors perform the same task and compare results, or certain specialized pipeline architectures like systolic arrays. However, its practical utility as a broad parallel processing paradigm is limited.\n\n**SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data)**\nSIMD architectures feature a single control unit that broadcasts the same instruction to multiple processing elements. Each processing element then executes this identical instruction simultaneously on a different piece of data. This model is highly efficient for data-parallel tasks where the same operation "
