struction address for its execution sequence. This output then becomes the input to the microprogram control unit's address register, effectively starting the execution of that specific instruction's microprogram. \n\nThe main advantage of this method is its flexibility and ease of design. Firstly, it simplifies the control logic significantly compared to using complex combinatorial circuits. Designers can easily change the microprogram sequence for an instruction simply by reprogramming the ROM, without altering the physical wiring or logic gates. This allows for easier error correction or feature additions (e.g., adding new instructions or modifying existing ones) during the design phase or even after manufacturing if the ROM is reprogrammable (like EPROM or flash memory). Secondly, it offers a systematic and organized way to manage the mapping, which can be particularly beneficial in complex instruction sets, as it directly translates the instruction's identity into the start of its execution flow.\nRubric for this sample:\n  1. (2 marks) Explanation that the instr"
