nds.\n\n    MOV R1, A        ; R1 = A\n    ADD R1, B        ; R1 = R1 + B (R1 = A + B)\n    MOV R2, C        ; R2 = C\n    ADD R2, D        ; R2 = R2 + D (R2 = C + D)\n    MUL R1, R2       ; R1 = R1 * R2 (R1 = (A+B)*(C+D))\n    MOV X, R1        ; X = R1\n\n3.  **One-address instruction (Accumulator-based):**\n    These instructions use an implicit accumulator register (AC) for one of the operands and to store the result. Explicit memory operands are used for loading and storing.\n\n    LOAD A           ; AC = A\n    ADD B            ; AC = AC + B (AC = A + B)\n    STORE TEMP1      ; TEMP1 = AC\n    LOAD C           ; AC = C\n    ADD D            ; AC = AC + D (AC = C + D)\n    MUL TEMP1        ; AC = AC * TEMP1 (AC = (C+D)*(A+B))\n    STORE X          ; X = AC\n\n4.  **Zero-address instruction (Stack-based):**\n    This architecture uses a stack to implicitly hold operands. Operations pop operands from the top of the stack, perform the operation, and push the result back onto the stack.\n\n    PUSH A           ; Stack: [A]\n    PUSH B           ; Stack: [A, B]\n    ADD              ; Pop B, Pop A, "
