comes $\\int_{-1}^1 |x| x dx$.\\nDue to the absolute value function, we split the integral:\\n$\\int_{-1}^1 |x| x dx = \\int_{-1}^0 (-x) x dx + \\int_0^1 (x) x dx$\\n$= \\int_{-1}^0 -x^2 dx + \\int_0^1 x^2 dx$\\n$= [-\\frac{x^3}{3}]_{-1}^0 + [\\frac{x^3}{3}]_0^1$\\n$= (-\\frac{0^3}{3} - (-\\frac{(-1)^3}{3})) + (\\frac{1^3}{3} - \\frac{0^3}{3})$\\n$= (0 - (-\\frac{-1}{3})) + (\\frac{1}{3} - 0)$\\n$= (0 - \\frac{1}{3}) + \\frac{1}{3}$\\n$= -\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} = 0$.\\nSo, $\\int_{C_1} |z| \\bar{z} dz = 0$.\\n\\nStep 3: Evaluate the integral over the upper half circle $C_2$.\\nOn $C_2$, $|z|=1$. We parameterize $z$ as $z = e^{i\\theta}$.\\nSince it's the upper half of the circle, positively oriented, $\\theta$ ranges from $0$ to $\\pi$ (starting from $z=1$ to $z=-1$).\\nFor this parameterization:\\n$|z|=1$.\\n$\\bar{z} = \\overline{e^{i\\theta}} = e^{-i\\theta}$.\\n$dz = \\frac{d}{d\\theta}(e^{i\\theta}) d\\theta = i e^{i\\theta} d\\theta$.\\nSubstituting these into the integral:\\n$\\int_{C_2} |z| \\bar{z} dz = \\int_0^\\pi (1) (e^{-i\\theta}) (i e^{i\\theta}) d\\theta$\\n$= \\int_0^\\pi i e^{(-i\\theta + i\\theta)} d\\theta$\\n$= \\int_0^\\pi i e^0"
